---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const templateUrl =
  'https://docs.google.com/document/d/18y_xQoj1MG0fgexS754-vcF1TN2MfYc2GYY9ep0aMzY/edit';
const signupSheetUrl =
  'https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1sMP_UmzB7Fb_SaUf3JWwVCtHyxsMyBOVJUVzAclZ2WM/edit?usp=sharing';
const mailto =
  'mailto:glen.w.wright@gmail.com?subject=' +
  encodeURIComponent('Little Blue Letter — edition draft');
---

<Base
  title="How to write an edition"
  description="Content, length, and submission notes for Little Blue Letter authors."
  path="/write/instructions/"
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="card write instructions">
      <p class="crumb"><a href="/write/">Authors</a></p>
      <h1>How to write an edition</h1>
      <p>
        Little Blue Letter is a splash of uplifting ocean stuff — not a big briefing or a
        literature review. Skim a few issues in the <a href="/archive/">archive</a> to get a sense
        of the style and content.
      </p>

      <h2>How long?</h2>
      <p>
        There is no hard word limit. A typical edition is around <strong>300–500 words</strong>. It
        can be short for a simple ocean update, or longer if you have a story to tell. Readers
        should be able to finish it with a cup of tea before the kettle cools ☕
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Ocean updates:</strong> 2–3 items; each is usually a few sentences totalling
          <strong>80–120 words</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Optional extras:</strong> one or two, if you like; a couple of bullets or about
          <strong>50–80 words</strong> each is plenty.
        </li>
        <li>
          Bullet points are fine. A finished draft is lovely; a well-chosen pile of notes is also
          enough.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What to send</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Open the
          <a href={templateUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Google Doc template</a>,
          then <strong>File → Make a copy</strong> (or just email the same sections without the
          doc).
        </li>
        <li>
          Fill in the sections below. Keep it concise, uplifting, and a little light-hearted.
        </li>
        <li>
          Put everything in <a href={mailto}>one email to glen.w.wright@gmail.com</a>.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Every edition needs a quote, ocean update(s) and your bio. Most letters include one or two
        of the optional sections below and a GIF.
      </p>
      <p>
        Claim a Friday on the
        <a href={signupSheetUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">sign-up sheet</a>
        if you have not already.
      </p>

      <h2>The pieces</h2>
      <h3>A quote</h3>
      <p>An inspirational ocean line (preferably not too cheesy!)</p>

      <h3>Ocean update</h3>
      <p>
        Short paragraphs on current or recent ocean news and research. Aim for a positive angle, or
        a promising / interesting way through a gloomy problem. Provide source links. A credited
        photo for at least one of the stories is appreciated.
      </p>

      <h3>Gif of the week</h3>
      <p>
        An ocean-themed animated gif (see e.g.,
        <a href="https://giphy.com/search/ocean" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Giphy</a>)
        and one short sentence of explanation if it needs one.
      </p>

      <h3>Optional extras (pick one or two)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Ocean Optimism:</strong> success stories, innovations, or conservation projects.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Further reading:</strong> papers or longer articles that expand on something in
          your ocean updates.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Book Club:</strong> an ocean-y book you recently read and enjoyed.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Meanwhile, on land:</strong> a general news story that still touches the ocean —
          land-based pollution, policy, behavioural economics…
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Ocean opportunity:</strong> a youth summit, fellowship, job, deadline…
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Your bio</h3>
      <p>
        Two or three sentences: <em>Little Blue Letter was curated this week by [full name].
        [First name] is [role] at [place]. [A line about research, interests, or why the ocean has
        you.]</em> Send a profile pic and any links you want included (LinkedIn, personal web
        page etc.).
      </p>

      <h2>Tone</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Write as if you are telling a friend something good (or at least interesting) that
          happened at sea this week.
        </li>
        <li>Plain language. One idea per item. We like a well-placed emoji!</li>
        <li>We edit, so you can focus on the content and your voice/story :)</li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
